\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces The strain Sensitivity of the aLIGO and A+ detectors. The noise due to radiation pressure and photon shot noise is shown in dark-blue (aLIGO) and dark-red (A+). Above 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, quantum effects are one of the limiting sources of noise; with the other being the noise due to Brownian motion of the mirror's coating. Below 10\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, seismic noise begins to dominate the noise of the detector. Above 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, shot noise dominates the noise of the detector. This rises with $f$ as the shot noise is filtered by the arm cavities. Below 100\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}Hz, the quantum noise goes as $1/f$ due to radiation pressure.\relax }}{15}{figure.caption.16}}
\newlabel{fig:detectorchapterdesignsensitivitycurves}{{1.3}{15}{The strain Sensitivity of the aLIGO and A+ detectors. The noise due to radiation pressure and photon shot noise is shown in dark-blue (aLIGO) and dark-red (A+). Above 10\,Hz, quantum effects are one of the limiting sources of noise; with the other being the noise due to Brownian motion of the mirror's coating. Below 10\,Hz, seismic noise begins to dominate the noise of the detector. Above 100\,Hz, shot noise dominates the noise of the detector. This rises with $f$ as the shot noise is filtered by the arm cavities. Below 100\,Hz, the quantum noise goes as $1/f$ due to radiation pressure.\relax }{figure.caption.16}{}}
